===Defeats===
Two Republicans sought reelection, but lost in the primary election. The last elected incumbent senator to lose renomination was [[Richard Lugar]] in [[2012 United States Senate election in Indiana|2012]],{{efn|name=PrimaryLoss|Republican senator [[Luther Strange]] ran to complete the term he was appointed to in [[2017 United States Senate special election in Alabama|2017]], but lost the primary to [[Roy Moore]], who narrowly lost the general election to Democratic nominee [[Doug Jones (politician)|Doug Jones]].}} and the last time multiple elected incumbent senators lost renomination in primaries was in [[2010 United States Senate elections|2010]].{{efn|Two elected incumbent senators lost renomination in primaries in 2010: [[Lisa Murkowski]] in [[2010 United States Senate election in Alaska|Alaska]] and [[Arlen Specter]] in [[2010 United States Senate Democratic primary election in Pennsylvania|Pennsylvania]]. After losing her primary, Murkowski ran a write-in campaign during the general election and won. In addition, [[Bob Bennett (politician)|Bob Bennett]] lost renomination at the state convention in [[2010 United States Senate election in Utah|Utah]].}} The last incumbent senator to place no better than third in a primary before Cassidy was [[Hattie Caraway]] in [[1944 United States Senate election in Arkansas|1944]].<ref name="SittingSenatorsPlaceThird">{{cite web|url=https://smartpolitics.lib.umn.edu/2026/02/09/how-often-do-sitting-us-senators-place-third-or-worse-in-primaries/|title=How Often Do Sitting US Senators Place Third (Or Worse) in Primaries?|publisher=Smart Politics|date=February 9, 2026|access-date=February 14, 2026}}</ref>{{efn|Republican senator [[Maurice J. Murphy Jr.]] ran to complete the term he was appointed to in [[1962 United States Senate special election in New Hampshire|1962]] but placed third in the primary.<ref name="SittingSenatorsPlaceThird" />}}
