[[Florida House of Representatives|State Representative]] [[Angie Nixon]]<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 15, 2026 |title=Florida Senate candidate Angie Nixon promises to fight for working people |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/miami/news/florida-democratic-senate-candidate-angie-nixon-campaign/ |access-date=March 20, 2026 |publisher=CBS News |language=en-US}}</ref> defeated [[Alex Vindman]], former director for European affairs at the [[United States National Security Council|U.S. National Security Council]], a [[Whistleblowing|whistleblower]] in the [[2019 Trump–Ukraine scandal]], and the twin brother of [[Virginia]] [[U.S. Representative]] [[Eugene Vindman]]<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://floridaphoenix.com/2026/01/27/whistleblower-alexander-vindman-to-seek-florida-democratic-nomination-for-senate/ |title=Whistleblower Alexander Vindman to seek Florida Democratic nomination for Senate |date=January 27, 2026 |access-date=January 30, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=DeFede |first=Jim |date=May 16, 2025 |title=Trump whistleblower Alexander Vindman eyeing Florida Senate run |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/miami/news/trump-whistleblower-alexander-vindman-eyeing-florida-senate-run/ |access-date=May 31, 2025 |publisher=CBS News |language=en-US}}</ref> in the August 18 Democratic primary.
