{{Main|2026 United States Senate election in Kentucky}}

{{See also|List of United States senators from Kentucky}}
Seven-term Republican and former [[Party leaders of the United States Senate|Senate Republican Leader]] [[Mitch McConnell]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in Kentucky|reelected in 2020]] with 57.8% of the vote. McConnell retired as leader after the 2024 elections with plans to serve out the remainder of his term.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bolton |first=Alexander |date=April 2, 2024 |title=McConnell will serve out full term through 2026 |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/4569800-mcconnell-will-serve-out-full-term-through-2026/ |access-date=August 7, 2024 |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref> On February 20, 2025, McConnell announced he would not seek reelection.<ref name="McConnell">{{cite web |last1=Schreiner |first1=Bruce |title=Sen. Mitch McConnell won't seek reelection in 2026, ending long tenure as Republican power broker|url=https://apnews.com/article/mitch-mcconnell-senate-retirement-34c79ef12bf62d14cb71d3c393f23a83 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=February 20, 2025 |date=February 20, 2025}}</ref>

On May 19, U.S. Representative [[Andy Barr]] of [[Kentucky's 6th congressional district]]<ref name="Barr">{{cite news |last1=Desrochers |first1=Daniel |last2=Goodman |first2=Jasper |title=Andy Barr to launch Senate bid to succeed Mitch McConnell |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/17/andy-barr-kentucky-senate-mitch-mcconnell-00298202 |access-date=April 17, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=April 17, 2025}}</ref> defeated a crowded field that included former [[Kentucky Attorney General]] and [[2023 Kentucky gubernatorial election|2023 Republican gubernatorial nominee]] [[Daniel Cameron (American politician)|Daniel Cameron]]<ref name="DanielCameron">{{cite news |last1=Horn |first1=Austin |title=Daniel Cameron launches 2026 U.S. Senate bid on heels of Mitch McConnell announcement |url=https://www.kentucky.com/news/politics-government/article300667499.html |access-date=February 20, 2025 |work=[[Lexington Herald-Leader]] |date=February 20, 2025}}</ref> for the Republican nomination.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |date=May 19, 2026 |title=2026 Kentucky U.S. Senate Primary Election Live Results |url=https://apnews.com/projects/elections-2026/kentucky-primary-results-us-senate/ |access-date=May 20, 2026 |publisher=Associated Press |language=en}}</ref>

Former [[Kentucky House of Representatives|state Representative]] [[Charles Booker (American politician)|Charles Booker]], the [[2022 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2022 Senate]] nominee and a former staffer for Governor [[Andy Beshear]], won the May 19 Democratic primary.<ref name="kentuckylantern.com">{{Cite web |title=Kentucky Democrat Charles Booker to run again for U.S. Senate seat • Kentucky Lantern |url=https://kentuckylantern.com/briefs/kentucky-democrat-charles-booker-to-run-again-for-u-s-senate-seat/ |access-date=March 5, 2026 |website=Kentucky Lantern |date=December 3, 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref> The seven-candidate field included [[2020 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2020 nominee]] and former [[United States Marine Corps|Marine]] fighter pilot [[Amy McGrath]].<ref name="kentuckylantern.com" /><ref name=":4" />
