{{Main|2026 United States Senate election in Louisiana}}

{{See also|List of United States senators from Louisiana}}
Two-term Republican [[Bill Cassidy]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in Louisiana|reelected in 2020]] with 59.3% of the vote in the first round of the "[[Louisiana primary]]" and ran for reelection to a third term, but placed a distant third in the jungle primary.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hilburn |first1=Greg |title=Bill Cassidy discusses reelection plans while blistering Louisiana closed party primary law |url=https://www.shreveporttimes.com/story/news/2024/01/30/will-louisiana-senator-bill-cassidy-run-for-reelection-under-new-closed-party-primary-law-he-hates/72405214007/ |access-date=January 31, 2024 |work=[[The Times (Shreveport)|The Times]] |date=January 30, 2024}}</ref> The Louisiana primary system had since been eliminated in favor of partisan primaries, which were closed to members of other parties but open to unaffiliated voters.<ref name="li">{{Cite web |last=Muller |first=Wesley |date=January 19, 2024 |title=Gov. Landry, lawmakers disrupt how Louisiana has voted for nearly 50 years |url=https://lailluminator.com/2024/01/19/closed-primaries-2/ |access-date=November 23, 2024 |website=Louisiana Illuminator}}</ref>

U.S. Representative [[Julia Letlow]] and [[Louisiana State Treasurer|state Treasurer]] and former U.S. representative [[John Fleming (Louisiana politician)|John Fleming]] advanced to the Republican runoff, defeating Cassidy in the primary.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Howard |first1=Andrew |last2=Doherty |first2=Erin |last3=Hill |first3=Meredith Lee |title=Letlow launches Trump-backed Senate bid against Cassidy |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/01/20/letlow-senate-cassidy-trump-00736200 |access-date=January 20, 2026 |work=[[Politico]] |date=January 20, 2026}}</ref><ref name="BC">{{Cite web |title=Treasurer John Fleming announces bid for Bill Cassidy's Senate seat in 2026 |url=https://www.wbrz.com/news/treasurer-john-fleming-announces-bid-for-bill-cassidy-s-senate-seat-in-2026 |access-date=December 4, 2024 |publisher=WBRZ}}</ref> Letlow, who was endorsed by President [[Donald Trump]], defeated Fleming in the runoff.<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 20, 2026 |title=Rep. Julia Letlow enters the Louisiana Senate race following Trump's endorsement |url=https://www.wwltv.com/article/news/local/rep-julia-letlow-enters-the-louisiana-senate-race-following-trumps-endorsement-politics-senate-race/289-6e39d0f3-4410-47a8-b457-ff4ed4dcf8ec |access-date=January 20, 2026 |publisher=WWL-TV |language=en-US}}</ref>

Sorghum farmer Jamie Davis and data scientist and political consultant Gary Crockett advanced to the Democratic primary runoff, while nonprofit executive Nick Albares placed third.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Grant|first1=Jalon|title=Concordia Parish native announces U.S. Senate bid in Louisiana |url=https://www.cenlanow.com/news/state-news/concordia-parish-native-announces-u-s-senate-bid-in-louisiana/ |access-date=December 12, 2025 |publisher=[[WNTZ-TV]] |date=December 12, 2025}}</ref> Davis defeated Crockett in the runoff.<ref name="Primary">{{cite web |title=Rancor ratchets up between Cassidy, Letlow with the US Senate field set |url=https://lailluminator.com/2026/02/13/cassidy-letlow-senate/ |website=Louisiana Illuminator |date=February 13, 2026|access-date=February 17, 2026}}</ref>
