Two-term Democrat [[Gary Peters (politician)|Gary Peters]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in Michigan|narrowly reelected in 2020]] with 49.9% of the vote. On January 28, 2025, he announced that he would not seek reelection.<ref name="GaryPeters">{{cite news |last1=Burke |first1=Melissa Nann |title=Michigan's Gary Peters won't seek reelection to U.S. Senate. Here's why |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2025/01/28/michigan-democratic-senator-gary-peters-will-not-run-for-reelection-to-us-senate-seat-2026/77985876007/ |access-date=January 28, 2025 |work=The Detroit News |date=January 28, 2025}}</ref>

Former [[Wayne County, Michigan|Wayne County]] Health, Human, and Veterans Services Department Director [[Abdul El-Sayed]],<ref name="El Sayed">{{cite news |last1=Wu |first1=Nicholas |title=Abdul El-Sayed launches Michigan Senate campaign |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/17/abdul-el-sayed-michigan-senate-campaign-00293627 |access-date=April 17, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=April 17, 2025}}</ref> U.S. Representative [[Haley Stevens]] of the [[Michigan's 11th congressional district|11th congressional district]]<ref name=":hstevens">{{Cite web |last=Wu |first=Nicholas |date=April 22, 2025 |title=Haley Stevens launches Senate bid in Michigan |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/22/haley-stevens-launches-senate-bid-00301416 |access-date=April 22, 2025 |website=[[Politico]] |language=en}}</ref>, and [[Michigan Senate|State Senator]] [[Mallory McMorrow]] ran in the Democratic primary. El-Sayed narrowly defeated Stevens and is the Democratic nominee.

Former U.S. Representative for the [[Michigan's 8th congressional district|8th congressional district]] and [[2024 United States Senate election in Michigan|2024]] U.S. Senate nominee [[Mike Rogers (Michigan politician)|Mike Rogers]]<ref name="MRogers">{{Cite news |last=Robinson |first=Taylor |date=April 14, 2025 |title=Mike Rogers Runs for Senate in Michigan After a Close 2024 Loss |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/14/us/politics/mike-rogers-senate-michigan.html |access-date=April 14, 2025 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> is the Republican nominee.
