One-term Democrat [[Tina Smith]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in Minnesota|reelected in 2020]] with 48.7% of the vote after being appointed by Governor [[Mark Dayton]] in 2018 following [[Al Franken]]'s resignation and then winning a [[2018 United States Senate special election in Minnesota|special election]] that year. On February 13, 2025, she announced she would not seek a second full term in 2026.<ref name="tinasmith">{{cite news |last1=Bakst |first1=Brian |title=Sen. Tina Smith won't seek reelection in 2026, putting Minnesota seat up for grabs |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2025/02/13/tina-smith-will-not-run-for-reelection-in-senate-in-2026 |access-date=February 13, 2025 |publisher=[[Minnesota Public Radio]] |date=February 13, 2025}}</ref> Incumbent [[Lieutenant Governor of Minnesota|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Peggy Flanagan]] announced her candidacy the same day.<ref name="FlanaganMN">{{cite news |title=MN Lt. Gov. Peggy Flanagan 'intends' to run for US Senate |url=https://www.fox9.com/news/peggy-flanagan-us-senate-minnesota |access-date=February 13, 2025 |publisher=FOX 9 |date=February 13, 2025}}</ref> [[U.S. Representative]] [[Angie Craig]] announced her candidacy on April 29.<ref name="CraigMN">{{Cite web |date=April 29, 2025 |title=Rep. Angie Craig launches Senate run in Minnesota ahead of a competitive Democratic primary |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/rep-angie-craig-launches-senate-run-minnesota-ahead-competitive-democr-rcna203371 |access-date=April 29, 2025 |publisher=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> Flanagan won the nomination with 59% of the vote.

Republicans running included [[sportscaster]] [[Michele Tafoya]],<ref name="Tafoya">{{Cite web |date=January 21, 2026 |title=Former sports reporter Michele Tafoya running for U.S. Senate in Minnesota - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/michele-tafoya-files-to-run-u-s-senate-minnesota/ |access-date=January 21, 2026 |publisher=CBS News |language=en-US}}</ref> [[2024 United States Senate election in Minnesota|2024]] Republican U.S. Senate nominee and former professional basketball player [[Royce White]],<ref name="RoyceWhiteMN">{{Cite web|url=https://kstp.com/kstp-news/local-news/royce-white-fresh-off-loss-to-amy-klobuchar-announces-he-will-run-for-senate-again-in-2026/|title=Royce White, fresh off loss to Amy Klobuchar, announces he will run for Senate again in 2026|first=Kyle|last=Brown|date=November 7, 2024|publisher=KSTP |access-date=February 23, 2025}}</ref> retired [[U.S. Navy]] officer Tom Weiler,<ref name="rn">{{cite news |last1=Brown |first1=Kyle |title=Navy veteran Tom Weiler to seek Republican nomination for open U.S. Senate seat |url=https://kstp.com/kstp-news/local-news/navy-veteran-tom-weiler-to-seek-republican-nomination-for-open-u-s-senate-seat/ |access-date=August 11, 2025 |publisher=[[KSTP-TV]] |date=August 11, 2025}}</ref> and former [[Navy SEAL]] Adam Schwarze.<ref name="Schwarze">{{cite news |last1=Frey |first1=Josh |title=Adam Schwarze announces candidacy for U.S. Senate in Minnesota to replace Tina Smith |url=https://www.kaaltv.com/news/adam-schwarze-announces-candidacy-for-u-s-senate-in-minnesota-to-replace-tina-smith/ |access-date=February 13, 2025 |publisher=[[KAAL-TV]] |date=February 13, 2025}}</ref> Tafoya ultimately won the nomination with 52% of the vote.
