Two-term Republican [[Ben Sasse]] resigned early in the [[118th United States Congress|118th Congress]] to become president of the [[University of Florida]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=Emanuel|first=Mike|date=October 6, 2022|title=Sasse resigning Senate seat, likely to be next University of Florida president-US|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/sasse-resigning-senate-seat-likely-next-university-florida-president}}</ref> On January 12, 2023, Governor [[Jim Pillen]] appointed former governor [[Pete Ricketts]] as senator.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Everett|first1=Burgess|last2=Levine|first2=Marianne|title=Sasse expected to resign from Senate|website=[[Politico]]|date=October 5, 2022|url= https://www.politico.com/news/2022/10/06/sasse-expected-to-resign-from-senate-00060812}}</ref> Ricketts won the [[2024 United States Senate special election in Nebraska|2024 special election]] to complete Sasse's term, defeating college professor [[Preston Love Jr.]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/projects/election-results-2024/nebraska/?r=82986|title=Nebraska Senate Special|date=November 5, 2024|publisher=Associated Press}}</ref> and is now running for reelection to his first full term.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Weaver |first1=Al |title=Former Nebraska Gov. Pete Ricketts tapped to fill Sasse's Senate seat |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/3805689-former-nebraska-gov-pete-ricketts-tapped-to-fill-sasses-senate-seat/ |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=January 12, 2023 |access-date=January 12, 2023 |quote=Pillen also noted that Ricketts has committed to running in 2024 and 2026}}</ref>

Former labor union leader [[Dan Osborn]], who challenged Republican [[Deb Fischer]] for [[2024 United States Senate election in Nebraska|Nebraska's Class I Senate seat]], has announced an independent bid.<ref name="Osborn">{{cite news |last1=Vakil |first1=Caroline |title=Dan Osborn launches independent Senate bid against Ricketts in Nebraska |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5389147-dan-osborn-independent-senate-bid-nebraska/ |access-date=July 8, 2025 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=July 8, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> 

The [[Nebraska Democratic Party]] has declined to recruit a Democratic challenger to Ricketts,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nir |first=David |title=Morning Digest: Nebraska independent who spooked GOP launches another Senate bid |url=https://www.the-downballot.com/p/morning-digest-nebraska-independent |access-date=September 6, 2025 |website=The Downballot |date=July 9, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> and the [[Jane Kleeb|party's chair]] has endorsed Osborn.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nebraska Dems chair 'supporting' Dan Osborn vs. U.S. Sen. Pete Ricketts |url=https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/nebraska-dems-chair-supporting-dan-osborn-vs-us-sen-pete-ricketts |access-date=September 6, 2025 |website=News From The States |language=en}}</ref> Other candidates filed for the Democratic primary, raising concern that their campaigns could split the vote and weaken Osborn's support in the general election,<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=March 9, 2026 |title=A slew of indie candidates are running for Senate in deep-red states. Democrats aren't all thrilled. |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/09/a-slew-of-indie-candidates-are-running-for-senate-in-deep-red-states-democrats-arent-all-thrilled-00817728 |access-date=March 16, 2026 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> but retired pharmacy technician Cindy Burbank won the Democratic primary and said she intended to withdraw from the general election if she sees no clear path to victory.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 13, 2026 |title=Democrats pick Nebraska Senate nominee who could drop out and back independent Dan Osborn |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/democrats-nebraska-senate-primary-dan-osborn-pete-ricketts-rcna344474 |access-date=May 28, 2026 |publisher=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> She officially withdrew from the race on July 17.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.ketv.com/article/nebraska-senate-democrat-candidate-cindy-burbank-ballot-removal/73172106 |title=Cindy Burbank, Democratic nominee for U.S. Senate in Nebraska, asks to be removed from ballot |last=Anderson |first=Jake |publisher=[[KETV]] |date=July 17, 2026 |access-date=July 17, 2026}}</ref>
