Two-term Democrat [[Cory Booker]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in New Jersey|reelected in 2020]] with 57.2% of the vote and is running for reelection to a third full term.<ref name="BookerRunning">{{cite web|url=https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/booker-says-no-to-possible-run-for-n-j-governor/|title=Booker says no to possible run for N.J. governor|last=Wildstein|first=David|date=February 14, 2022|access-date=February 14, 2022|website=[[New Jersey Globe]]}}</ref> Booker won the Democratic nomination unopposed, as he was the only candidate to submit the required number of signatures to appear on the primary ballot.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NJ DOS - Division of Elections - 2026 Election Information and Results |url=https://www.nj.gov/state/elections/election-information-2026.shtml |access-date=March 24, 2026 |website=nj.gov}}</ref>

On June 2, 2026, former [[Tabernacle Township, New Jersey|Tabernacle]] Mayor Justin Murphy, a Navy veteran and attorney, won the Republican nomination<ref>{{Cite web |title=Justin Murphy wins New Jersey's Republican Senate primary |url=https://whyy.org/articles/new-jersey-election-2026-primary-senate-republican-nomination/ |access-date=June 3, 2026 |publisher=WHYY |language=en-US}}</ref> with 33% of the vote, defeating physician Robert Lebovics, U.S. Army combat veteran Richard Tabor, and former [[News 12 Networks|News 12]] reporter Alex Zdan.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wildstein |first=David |date=February 21, 2026 |title=Tabor defeats Zdan for U.S. Senate endorsement in Atlantic |url=https://newjerseyglobe.com/congress/tabor-defeats-zdan-for-u-s-senate-endorsement-in-atlantic/ |access-date=March 16, 2026 |website=New Jersey Globe |language=en-US}}</ref>{{clear}}

