Two-term Republican [[Thom Tillis]], who was [[2020 United States Senate election in North Carolina|narrowly reelected in 2020]] with 48.7% of the vote, announced on June 29, 2025, that he would not seek a third term after facing backlash for voting against [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act]], prompting [[Donald Trump]] to call for a primary challenger.<ref name="Tillis" /> After Tillis withdrew, Trump endorsed former [[Republican National Committee]] chair [[Michael Whatley]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 31, 2025 |title=Trump-backed RNC Chair Michael Whatley launches North Carolina Senate bid |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/5426353-rnc-chair-michael-whatley-nc-senate/ |access-date=April 10, 2026 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref> who won the Republican nomination, defeating [[Don Brown (author, born 1960)|Don Brown]] in the March 3 primary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gallagher |date=March 4, 2026 |title=Roy Cooper and Michael Whatley will advance to a marquee North Carolina matchup for US Senate |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/03/politics/roy-cooper-michael-whatley-north-carolina-matchup-senate |access-date=March 4, 2026 |publisher=[[CNN]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

[[Lara Trump]], former co-chair of the [[Republican National Committee]] and [[Wilmington, North Carolina|Wilmington]] native, was considered a potential candidate to challenge Tillis, but announced she would not run.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Walker |first=Josephine |date=July 24, 2025 |title=Lara Trump won't run for Sen. Thom Tillis' seat in North Carolina |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/07/24/lara-trump-declines-north-carolina-senate-seat |access-date=September 6, 2025 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, former [[U.S. Representative]] [[Wiley Nickel]] declared his candidacy in April 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wu |first=Nicholas |date=April 9, 2025 |title=Former Rep. Wiley Nickel is launching a Senate bid in North Carolina — giving hope to Democrats |url=https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2025/04/09/congress/wiley-nickel-senate-north-carolina-00279297 |access-date=April 9, 2025 |website=[[Politico]]}}</ref> Former [[North Carolina Governor|Governor]] [[Roy Cooper]] entered the race on July 28, 2025.<ref name="nc" /> Nickel withdrew the following day and endorsed Cooper,<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 29, 2025 |title=Former Congressman Wiley Nickel drops out of 2026 Senate race, endorses Roy Cooper |url=https://abc11.com/post/former-congressman-wiley-nickel-drops-2026-senate-race-endorses-north-carolina-governor-roy-cooper/17348739/ |access-date=July 29, 2025 |publisher=ABC11 |location=Raleigh-Durham |language=en}}</ref> who later won the Democratic nomination.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=March 3, 2026 |title=Democrat Roy Cooper and Republican Michael Whatley set to face off in key North Carolina Senate race |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/cooper-whatley-north-carolina-senate-primary-wins-election-race-rcna259989 |access-date=March 4, 2025 |publisher=[[NBC News]] |language=en}}</ref>
