One-term Republican [[JD Vance]] was [[2022 United States Senate election in Ohio|elected in 2022]] with 53% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bradner |first1=Eric |last2=Main |first2=Alison |date=November 6, 2024 |title=Trump critic-turned-ally JD Vance elected vice president, offering glimpse at GOP's potential future |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/11/06/politics/jd-vance-elected-vice-president/index.html |access-date=November 7, 2024 |publisher=[[CNN]]}}</ref> On January 10, 2025, he resigned from the Senate after being elected [[Vice President of the United States]] in [[2024 United States presidential election|2024]]. On January 17, [[Ohio Governor|Governor]] [[Mike DeWine]] announced that then-[[Ohio Lieutenant Governor|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Jon Husted]] would replace Vance in the Senate.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=O'Keefe |first1=Ed |last2=Gómez |first2=Fin |last3=Navarro |first3=Aaron |last4=Watson |first4=Kathryn |date=January 17, 2025 |title=Jon Husted, Ohio's lieutenant governor, chosen to fill Vance's Senate seat |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/jon-husted-ohio-senate-seat-jd-vance/ |access-date=January 18, 2025 |publisher=CBS News}}</ref> Husted is running to serve the remainder of Vance's term.<ref name="Husted">{{cite news |last1=Smyth |first1=Julie Carr |title=Trump favorite Jon Husted launches 2026 U.S. Senate campaign in Ohio |url=https://apnews.com/article/election-2025-senate-ohio-husted-trump-67ed124a587b2265aaba273b2d5f3f43 |access-date=April 9, 2025 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |date=April 9, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

Former U.S. Senator [[Sherrod Brown]], who was unseated [[2024 United States Senate election in Ohio|in 2024]],<ref name="BrownAP">{{Cite web |date=August 18, 2025 |title=Ohio Democrat Sherrod Brown makes it official. He'll vie to unseat Trump-backed Sen. Jon Husted |url=https://apnews.com/article/election-2026-senate-ohio-sherrod-brown-trump-b47ba4a2a4da8e419de15047c33baa50 |access-date=August 18, 2025 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |author=Carr Smyth, Julie |language=en}}</ref> defeated IT professional Ron Kincaid in the Democratic primary on May 5, 2026.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stein |first=Chris |date=May 6, 2026 |title=Sherrod Brown and Jon Husted set for Senate showdown after Ohio primary victories |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/may/05/jon-husted-sherrod-brown-ohio-senate-primary |access-date=May 6, 2026 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

Former [[Libertarian National Committee]] chair William Redpath is running as a Libertarian.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Henry |first=Megan |date=February 6, 2026 |title=Here are the candidates running for Ohio statewide office in 2026 |url=https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/02/06/here-are-the-candidates-running-for-ohio-statewide-office-in-2026/ |access-date=March 17, 2026 |website=Ohio Capital Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> Ohio Libertarians disqualified healthcare consultant Jeffrey Kanter from the Libertarian primary after he submitted hundreds of invalid signatures with his candidate petition.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jeremy Pelzer |first=cleveland com |date=March 19, 2026 |title=Libertarian U.S. Senate candidate Jeffrey Kanter disqualified from Ohio ballot |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/03/libertarian-us-senate-candidate-jeffrey-kanter-disqualified-from-ohio-ballot.html |access-date=March 24, 2026 |website=The Plain Dealer |language=en}}</ref>
