{{See also|List of United States senators from Oklahoma}}
Incumbent Republican [[Markwayne Mullin]] won [[2022 United States Senate special election in Oklahoma|a special election in 2022]] with 61.8% of the vote to complete the remainder of the term vacated by [[Jim Inhofe]], who resigned on January 3, 2023, due to declining health and died in 2024.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Martin|first=Jonathan|date=February 24, 2022|title=James Inhofe, Oklahoma Senator, Is Said to Plan an Early Retirement-US|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/24/us/politics/jim-inhofe-senate-retirement.html|access-date=February 25, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> 

Mullin initially announced plans to seek his first full term in office, but on March 5, 2026, President Trump announced his nomination of Mullin for [[United States Secretary of Homeland Security|Secretary of Homeland Security]]; the Senate confirmed Mullin on March 23.<ref name="Mullin">{{Cite news |last=Bustillo|first=Ximena|date=March 23, 2026|title=Markwayne Mullin confirmed as the next secretary of Homeland Security|url=https://www.npr.org/2026/03/23/g-s1-114813/markwayne-mullin-confirmed-homeland-security|access-date=March 24, 2026|publisher=NPR|language=en}}</ref> Governor [[Kevin Stitt]] appointed board member and former CEO of [[Williams Companies]] [[Alan Armstrong]] to replace Mullin in the Senate.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dowers |first=Graham |date=March 22, 2026 |title=Oklahoma Gov. Stitt expected to appoint Alan Armstrong to Senate, sources tell News On 6 |url=https://www.newson6.com/national-politics-oklahoma/oklahoma-stitt-armstrong-senate-appointment |access-date=March 24, 2026 |website=NewsOn6.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Armstrong was sworn in on March 24<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 24, 2026 |title=Oklahoma energy executive Alan Armstrong sworn in as U.S. senator |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/03/24/mullins-senate-replacement-armstrong/ |access-date=March 24, 2026 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> and was required to sign an oath stating that he would not run in the 2026 election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ramos |first=Lionel |date=March 5, 2026 |title=Markwayne Mullin tapped to lead Homeland Security. Here's what happens to his Senate seat |url=https://www.kosu.org/whats-next-after-mullin |access-date=March 24, 2026 |publisher=KOSU |language=en}}</ref><ref name="OK Statutes" />

U.S. Representative for {{ushr|OK|1}} [[Kevin Hern]]<ref name="Hern11">{{cite web |last1=Fortinsky|first1=Sarah|title=Hern launches Senate bid for Mullin's Oklahoma seat|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5779166-hern-oklahoma-senate-bid/|website=The Hill|access-date=March 11, 2026|date=March 11, 2026}}</ref> won the June 16 Republican primary with 70% of the vote in a crowded field.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=April 3, 2026 |title=Candidates for Elective Office 2026 |url=https://oklahoma.gov/content/dam/ok/en/elections/candidate-filing-archives/2026-candidate-filing-archives/2026-candidate-list-book.pdf |access-date=April 4, 2026 |publisher=State of Oklahoma}}</ref>

In the June 16 Democratic primary, mediator Jim Priest<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dowers|first1=Graham|title=Democrat Jim Priest enters U.S. Senate race against Mullin|url=https://www.newson6.com/politics/jim-priest-oklahoma-senate-democrat-mullin|access-date=January 17, 2026|publisher=[[News 9]]|date=January 17, 2026}}</ref> and nurse N'Kiyla "Jasmine" Thomas<ref name="ThomasOK">{{cite web |last1=Fields |first1=Lace |date=July 25, 2025 |title=Democratic U.S. Senate candidate makes stop in McAlester |url=https://www.mcalesternews.com/community/democratic-u-s-senate-candidate-makes-stop-in-mcalester/article_86136e5d-af1c-4f53-8747-a5874f82c7ff.html |work=[[McAlester News-Capital]] |access-date=October 30, 2025}}</ref> received the most votes and advanced to an August 25 runoff.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 17, 2026 |title=Oklahoma Senate Primary Election 2026 Live Results |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/oklahoma-senate-results |access-date=June 17, 2026 |publisher=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> Thomas, a member of the [[Democratic Socialists of America]], won the runoff with 61% of the vote.

Entering Wedge Media executive director Ron Meinhardt and pharmacist Curtis Stinnett are running as independents.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Brumbelow |first=Cole |date=April 1, 2026 |title=Candidates file for local, county and statewide races in Oklahoma |url=https://www.kswo.com/2026/04/01/candidates-file-local-county-statewide-races-oklahoma/ |access-date=April 3, 2026 |publisher=KSWO |language=en}}</ref> Sevier White, candidate for [[Oklahoma's 4th congressional district]] in [[2016 United States House of Representatives elections in Oklahoma#District 4|2016]], is running as a [[Libertarian Party of Oklahoma|Libertarian]].<ref name=":2" />{{clear}}

