Two-term Republican [[Mike Rounds]], who was [[2020 United States Senate election in South Dakota|reelected in 2020]] with 65.7% of the vote, is running for a third term.<ref name="Rounds">{{cite web |last1=Hanson |first1=Tom |title=Mike Rounds says he has 'no plans' to leave Washington |date=August 29, 2025 |url=https://www.keloland.com/news/local-news/mike-rounds-says-he-has-no-plans-to-leave-washington/ |publisher=[[KELO-TV]] |access-date=August 30, 2025}}</ref> Rounds easily defeated Navy veteran and businessman Justin McNeil in the June 2 primary to win the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web |title=Campaign roundup: Republican announces for US Senate, other races grow, polls and money info emerge|url=https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/11/01/campaign-roundup-republican-announces-for-us-senate-other-races-grow-polls-and-money-info-emerge/|access-date=November 2, 2025|work=[[South Dakota Searchlight]]|date=November 1, 2025}}</ref>

Businessman and former [[South Dakota Highway Patrol|South Dakota state trooper]] Julian Beaudion was the Democratic nominee,<ref name="Beaudion">{{cite news |last1=Sneve |first1=Joe |title=From patrol to politics: Trooper-turned-activist enters South Dakota's U.S. Senate race |url=https://www.thedakotascout.com/p/from-patrol-to-politics-trooper-turned |access-date=April 10, 2025 |work=The Dakota Scout |date=April 10, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> but withdrew on August 4.<ref>{{cite news |title=Democrat exits Senate race in South Dakota, clearing way for independent |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/08/04/democrat-exits-senate-race-south-dakota-clearing-way-independent/ |access-date=August 4, 2026 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=August 4, 2026|first=Ben|last=Binday}}</ref>

Brian Bengs, a [[U.S. Navy]] and [[Air Force Specialty Code|Air Force]] veteran, former [[Northern State University]] political science professor, and the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee for U.S. Senate in [[2022 United States Senate election in South Dakota|2022]], has announced his candidacy as an independent.<ref name="Bengs">{{cite news |title=Brian Bengs announces independent run for U.S. Senate |url=https://www.dakotanewsnow.com/2025/04/30/brian-bengs-announces-independent-run-us-senate/ |access-date=April 30, 2025 |work=Dakota News Now |date=April 30, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>
