Four-term Republican [[John Cornyn]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in Texas|reelected in 2020]] with 53.5% of the vote, and ran for a fifth term in 2026.<ref name="JC">{{Cite web |date=November 29, 2024 |title=John Cornyn and Ken Paxton have been trading jabs as a potential primary showdown looms |url=https://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/2024/11/29/john-cornyn-and-ken-paxton-have-been-trading-jabs-as-a-potential-primary-showdown-looms/ |access-date=November 30, 2024 |website=Dallas Morning News|author=Jeffers, Gromer Jr.}}</ref>

Cornyn and [[Texas Attorney General|state Attorney General]] [[Ken Paxton]] ran in the Republican primary on March 3. Since neither received more than 50% of the vote, they advanced to a runoff election held on May 26.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Goodman |first1=J. David |title=Cornyn and Paxton Head to Runoff in Bitter Texas Senate Primary |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/03/us/elections/texas-senate-republican-primary-cornyn-paxton-runoff.html |access-date=March 4, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 3, 2026}}</ref> [[U.S. Representative]] for the [[Texas's 38th congressional district|38th congressional district]] [[Wesley Hunt]] challenged Paxton and Cornyn, but lost the primary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goodman |first=J. David |date=October 6, 2025 |title=Wesley Hunt leans into ugly Texas Senate race |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/06/us/politics/wesley-hunt-senate-texas-cornyn-paxton.html |access-date=October 6, 2025 |website=The New York Times |language=en}}</ref> On May 19, both Hunt and President Trump endorsed Paxton.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schneider |first=Andrew |date=May 19, 2026 |title=Trump picks Paxton over Cornyn in Texas' GOP Senate primary runoff as early voting is underway |url=https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/articles/news/politics/2026/05/19/552323/ken-paxton-trump-endorsement-texas-senate-republican-primary-runoff-cornyn/ |access-date=May 20, 2026 |publisher=Houston Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref> Paxton defeated Cornyn in the runoff by a landslide and became the Republican nominee.

[[Texas House of Representatives|State Representative]] [[James Talarico]] is the Democratic nominee, having defeated U.S. Representative [[Jasmine Crockett]] in the primary.<ref name="TalaricoTX">{{Cite web |last=Godfrey |first=Elaine |date=September 9, 2025 |title=The Millennial Seminarian Trying to Turn Texas Blue |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2025/09/james-talarico-texas-senate/684142/ |access-date=September 9, 2025 |website=The Atlantic |language=en}}</ref><ref name="CrockettTX">{{Cite web |date=December 8, 2025 |title=Rep. Jasmine Crockett launches Texas Senate run, shaking up Democratic primary |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/rep-jasmine-crockett-launches-texas-senate-run-shaking-democratic-prim-rcna247542 |access-date=December 8, 2025 |publisher=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> 

